**Abstract**: This chapter examines gender disparities in intellectual property (IP) awareness and participation, focusing on differences in subjective and objective knowledge of IP rights. Using survey data from 25,000 individuals across fifty countries, the chapter finds that women report lower familiarity with patents and trademarks but exhibit greater knowledge of copyrights and industrial designs. This finding highlights the need for policymakers to address IP awareness campaigns, considering gender differences in knowledge. These insights contribute to the broader discussion on the relationship between gender, innovation, and IP.
